<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import Snippet from '#src/lib/Snippet.abide'
import Answer from './lib/Answer.abide'
import answerSource from './lib/Answer.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import poolSource from './lib/pool.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import poolStateSource from './poolState.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import appTestSource from './tests/app.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import Pipeline from './lib/Pipeline.abide'
import pipelineSource from './lib/Pipeline.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import pipelineTestSource from './tests/Pipeline.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import DealStage from './lib/DealStage.abide'
import dealStageSource from './lib/DealStage.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import dealStageTestSource from './tests/DealStage.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`import { createApp } from '@abide/abide/server'

const app = await createApp()

const answer = await app.fetch(
    new Request('http://app.invalid/contacts/getContact?id=42'),
)`} />

    <p>It finds the app the way <code>abide start</code> does, from the directory it runs in, so run
    <code>bun test</code> from the app's own directory. Under <code>bun test</code> it builds the
    app from its sources as they stand, once, rather than reading whatever <code>abide build</code>
    last left.</p>

    <Section title="The app is an object, and a host is one caller">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th></th><th></th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>createApp()</code></td><td>tables, middleware, config, <code>onStart</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>app.fetch(request)</code></td><td>the whole request path, in this process</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'app.run(fn, { request })'}</code></td><td>one scope, with your function inside it</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'app.listen({ port })'}</code></td><td>the socket</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>app.stop()</code></td><td>drain, then <code>onStop</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><code>abide start</code> calls the first, then the fourth, then eventually the last. A
        test calls the first, then the second or the third, then the last. Same object, same route
        table, same rungs — what differs is that nothing is bound.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/ship/build-and-serve-the-app')}>Build &amp; start</a> ·
        <a href={url('/app/run-code-at-start-and-stop')}>Lifecycle</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`app.fetch` answers what the wire answers">
        <Example
            id="app-fetch"
            title="`createApp()` — the app a test holds, with no port"
            summary="The test beside the card asks for the pool through app.fetch, then stops the app. Fetch the same handler over the wire and read the same answer."
            files={{
                'lib/Answer.abide': answerSource,
                'poolState.ts': poolStateSource,
                'lib/pool.ts': poolSource,
                'tests/app.test.ts': appTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <Answer />
        </Example>

        <p>A <code>Request</code> in and a <code>Response</code> out, through everything a served
        request goes through: the middleware in registration order, the argument coercion, the
        timeout, the generated headers, and the status a refusal carries. The test asserts on the
        status and the <code>traceresponse</code> header, and the card reads the same two off the
        wire.</p>

        <p>Those are the parts a handler called directly does not have, which is the reason to reach
        for a request at all. Assert on the response and you are asserting on what a browser would
        have been sent.</p>

        <p><code>poolState.ts</code> is a handler file, so the address the test asks for is its
        path. The test is <code>tests/app.test.ts</code>, beside the page it tests: nothing under a
        <code>tests</code> directory has an address, so a test sits next to the page or the handler
        it reaches and is never served.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/ship/call-a-handler-from-the-terminal')}>Call a handler</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A test renders a `.abide` it imports">
        <Example
            id="component-in-a-test"
            title="`render(component)` — the `.abide` a test imports"
            summary="The pipeline sums the deals still open. Its test renders the same component and reads the total off the markup."
            files={{
                'lib/Pipeline.abide': pipelineSource,
                'tests/Pipeline.test.ts': pipelineTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <Pipeline />
        </Example>

        <p>The import is the component the build compiles, with no <code>createApp</code> run
        first, and <code>render</code> turns it into a document's bytes as a page's render does. The
        test asserts on markup, so what it checks is what a reader would see.</p>

        <p>One line in the app's <code>bunfig.toml</code> makes that so, and the scaffold writes
        it:</p>

        <Code language="toml" caption="bunfig.toml" code={`[test]
preload = ["@abide/abide/test"]`} />

        <p>Without it, <code>bun test</code> resolves a <code>.abide</code> import to the file's
        path, and the test fails on <code>Pipeline is not a function</code>.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/render-a-document-yourself')}>Manual rendering</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A test mounts a component and counts what a click did">
        <Example
            id="mount-in-a-test"
            title="`mount(component)` and `measure(body)` — a card a test clicks"
            summary="Advance moves the deal one stage. Its test mounts the same component, clicks Advance, and counts what the click wrote."
            files={{
                'lib/DealStage.abide': dealStageSource,
                'tests/DealStage.test.ts': dealStageTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <DealStage />
        </Example>

        <p><code>mount</code> renders the component the way the server does and hydrates it with
        the module the browser loads, so the test clicks the same listener a reader does.
        <code>measure</code> counts every DOM call its body makes, and abide's own counters beside
        them: <code>wakes</code> is effects that ran, and <code>bindingRuns</code> is template
        slots that reran.</p>

        <p>Assert the work as well as the text. A click that rebuilt the whole row would still
        read <code>qualified</code>; it would also report a <code>nodesCreated</code> above zero,
        and that is the line that fails.</p>

        <p>Both come from <code>@abide/harness</code>, and one preload sets them up: a DOM to mount
        into, then the <code>.abide</code> imports above. The scaffold writes it.</p>

        <Code language="toml" caption="bunfig.toml" code={`[test]
preload = ["@abide/harness/abide/preload"]`} />

        <p>The DOM under <code>bun test</code> is an emulator, so a count from it carries to a
        browser and a millisecond does not. Assert counts here, and time in a browser.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`app.run` is a scope and nothing else">
        <p>Calling a handler by name already runs its body, its rungs and its schema. What it needs
        from you is a scope — the thing a cache entry is filed under, and the lifetime a
        request-local value belongs to.</p>

        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`const loads = await app.run(async () => {
    await Promise.all([getContact({ id: '42' }), getContact({ id: '42' })])
    return database.contact.find.calls
})

expect(loads).toBe(1)`} />

        <p>Two reads of one key in one scope are one load, and a second <code>app.run</code> loads
        again. Your function's return value comes back out, so the assertion is on whatever you were
        watching.</p>

        <p>Hand it a request and the ambients come alive: <code>request()</code>,
        <code>cookies()</code> and <code>response()</code> read and write against the one you
        passed.</p>

        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`await app.run(() => getContact({ id: '42' }), {
    request: new Request('http://app.invalid/', {
        headers: { cookie: 'session=abc' },
    }),
})`} />

        <p>Leave it out and those three throw, which is what they do in a job or a script too.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/app/read-the-incoming-request')}>Request</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Bind a port only where a test needs the socket">
        <p><code>{'app.listen({ port: 0 })'}</code> takes whichever port is free, and that is the
        form to reach for when a test needs <code>server()</code> or a real <code>WebSocket</code>
        rather than a <code>Request</code>. Everything else stays off the network, so two test files
        holding an app are not queueing for the same number.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Stop the app, or the suite hangs">
        <p><code>app.stop()</code> stops accepting, waits for what is in flight, ends every stream
        it is still writing, closes its sockets and drops the seed entries its renders left, timers
        and all — and then <code>onStop</code> runs. A file that builds an app and never stops it
        leaves whatever <code>onStart</code> opened still open, and a suite that hangs reports
        nothing at all: no failure, no summary, nothing to read.</p>

        <p>Hold it with <code>await using</code> and it stops where the block ends, a failed
        assertion included. The test's second case stops the app by hand and finds the pool
        <code>onStart</code> opened closed again:</p>

        <Snippet source={appTestSource} path="tests/app.test.ts" from={['test(\'stopping']} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="There is no test client">
        <p>Nothing is generated for tests and there is nothing to keep in step. An rpc is one
        callable on both sides, and a rung runs on the in-process call as much as on the wire one —
        so what a test is missing was never a caller. It is a scope and a door, and those are
        <code>app.run</code> and <code>app.fetch</code>.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/read-data-without-writing-an-api')}>Reading data</a></p>
    </Section>
</Doc>
